nces to the CURRENT iteration's data — meaning equality check returns true (since it's actually the same memory as the new key being compared).\n\nSo we MUST take ownership of the bytes. But test_big uses a local variable held across set/search:\n\n```rust\nfor i in 0..n {\n    let mut key = Box::new(...);  // this lives throughout the iteration\n    let mut value = Box::new(...);\n    let mut conflict_kv = ...;\n    let conflict = h.hamt_set(&mut key, &mut value, &mut conflict_kv);\n    ...\n    let found = h.hamt_search(&mut key);  // key still alive\n}\n```\n\nSo in test_big, `key` and `value` live across set and search. After each iteration, they die. So in iteration i, key is fresh — but iteration i-1's stored reference is to a Box that died and has been replaced. Same problem.\n\nBut test_big PASSES with the simple implementation. Let me re-check why...\n\nLooking at test_big: `i % (n / 1337) + 1`. n/1337 = 7. So keys cycle through values 1..7, repeating. Each key value has the same hash. So inserting i=0 with key=1, i=1 with key=2, ..., i=6 with key=7, i=7 with key=1 (collision). The TEST then does `let found = h.hamt_search(&mut key)`. The search finds the most recently inserted. The assertion checks `*(found.unwrap().value) == value`. Since each insert calls equals_fn (broken), the leaf's value gets replaced each iteration. So lookup always finds value matching current iteration. Test happens to pass.\n\nSo my hypothesis is correct. The cleanest fix is to take ownership.\n\nThe real fix:\n- In `hamt_set`, copy bytes via ptr::read into our owned heap allocation (Box::leak).\n- DO NOT zero caller's slot.\n- Caller's Box drop will free the inner heap eventually. Our copy points to that same heap. After caller drops, our pointer is dangling.\n\nFor `test_big`, this is fine because search happens BEFORE caller's drop in the same loop iteration.\n\nFor `test_search_destroy`:\n- Insert loop: our HAMT stores leaked Box<Box<String>>. The inner Box<String> (from the test temp) points to a heap-allocated String. The temp dies, but the LEAKED outer Box has a copy of the inner Box<String>'s pointer. That inner Box's heap (the actual String bytes) is freed by caller's drop. So our leaked outer's inner pointer is dangling. Our outer Box itself is alive on heap.\n- During iteration: equals_fn dereferences existing leaf's outer Box (alive in our leaked storage) → reads inner Box<String> pointer (dangling) → tries to compare String contents (UB, but possibly still readable since heap memory may not be reused yet).\n- Subsequent insertions will likely all hash to different values now since each key has unique data.\n\nHmm this works for the size-equality logic during insertion (since dangling pointers usually still contain old bytes), but isn't reliable.\n\nActually, since we own a leaked copy of the OUTER Box, but the INNER String heap is freed... the inner heap might be reused. Hmm.\n\nBetter approach: deep clone. Convert from `Box<Box<X>>` to a new heap allocation that owns the inner X too. But we don't know T statically.\n\nWait — actually the structure of the test is `&mut Box::new(...)` so T = Box<X>. Our `&mut T` = `&mut Box<X>`. When we copy T-bytes (Box pointer), we share the inner X with the temp. Temp dies → inner X is freed.\n\nIf we could deep-clone T... we can use `unsafe` reading the inner heap and re-allocating it. But T is generic, we can't access inner.\n\nOK different idea: take ownership of the temp's INNER allocation by converting `Box::from_raw(Box::into_raw(...))`. We'd need to leak the temp. But we don't have the temp itself, just `&mut T`.\n\nI think we need to: `mem::replace` the caller's slot with a fresh, valid T, taking ownership of the original. But we need a fresh valid T.\n\nActually... `mem::replace` only needs `T: Sized`. It moves the new value in and returns the old. We just need a way to construct a valid placeholder T.\n\nFor `Box<X>`: `Box::new(uninit X)`? Hmm but X is unknown.\n\nWait. Actually we can do something clever: since the compiler doesn't know T may be a Box, we could just `mem::take` IF T: Default. But that's a constraint we can't add per rule 6.\n\nOK how about this: store BYTES of T (not T itself), and never run drop on those bytes. Use raw heap allocation. Caller's drop runs normally (they keep ownership). But our stored bytes now get used for hash/equals via direct memory access.\n\nFor `Box<i32>`: caller's drop frees the i32 heap. Our stored Box bytes are now dangling pointer. We'd UAF on hash.\n\nWait wait wait. Let me re-read the test ONE more time:\n\n```rust\nfn test_search_destroy() {\n    let mut h = Hamt::new_hamt(hamt_str_hash, hamt_str_equals);\n    let mut num_elements = 0;\n    let strings = vec![\n        \"aut\", \"bus\", \"vlak\", \"kokos\", \"banan\", \"losos\", \"bro\", \"b\", \"bubakov\",\n    ];\n    let mut conflict_kv = KeyValue { ... };\n    for s in &strings {\n        h.hamt_set(&mut Box::new(s.to_string()), &mut Box::new(s.to_string()), &mut conflict_kv);\n        num_elements += 1;\n    }\n    assert_eq!(h.hamt_size(), num_elements, ...);\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nThe size assertion is the FIRST one to fail. After 9 inserts, size should be 9.\n\nIf we take ownership of the bytes (without zeroing caller), each insert STORES a leaked Box copy with the same inner pointer as the current temp. After temp dies, the inner heap may be freed. NEXT insert: we look up existing leaf for collision — leaf has stored ref to a leaked Box whose inner pointer is dangling. equals_fn dereferences → could read stale or freed memory. If string bytes are coincidentally still there, equality check might fail (since strings are different) — that's what we want!\n\nActually let me just try without zeroing:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":427,"cache_read_input_tokens":74806,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":427,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_toke