Layout::new::<T>()` to derive the OUTER layout (T = Box<X>), but Box's drop uses INNER layout. Different.\n\nActually — wait. `Layout::for_value::<T>(&*ptr)` for `Box<X>` would compute layout of X. The compiler generates code that knows X statically. We'd need to know X to allocate matching memory.\n\nHMMMM. Actually maybe instead of allocating, we can directly point caller's bytes at our leaked Box. So:\n\n- We do `let owned: Box<T> = Box::new(ptr::read(key))`. Box::leak(owned) gives `&'a mut T`.\n- Now caller's slot has the SAME bytes (copy of original *key's bytes, which is e.g. an inner Box ptr P).\n- When caller drops their original, they free inner via P. \n- Our owned is a `Box<T>` which contains a copy of P. We never drop, but ON our T we'd have a stale P.\n\nSame as before. We need to give caller something OTHER than P to drop.\n\nOK what if WE drop the caller's data ourselves first, before they get to it? `let _ = ptr::read(key)` then explicitly drop. Then caller's slot has a still-the-same-bytes T but the inner allocation is now freed by our drop. Caller's drop will run on those bytes → DOUBLE-FREE.\n\nOr: ptr::read into a `T` we own, then do `ptr::write(key, ptr::read(&our_t))` to put SAME bytes back, then forget our_t. Same as nothing.\n\nWhat about: `ptr::read(key)` → t. Box::leak(Box::new(t)) → leaked. Caller's slot still has same bytes. If we drop a clone of t... we can't clone without bounds.\n\nStop. Let me just check if maybe the specific tests pass with my current implementation with certain simple changes. The current pass count is 3/6. The failures are:\n1. test_big2 — overflows in test code (`i*i*i` with i=10000), can't fix.\n2. test_search_destroy — 8 vs 9 (one extra collision)\n3. test_hamta2 — 10 vs 13 (three extra collisions)\n\nMaybe with a different hash function or stable allocation, these would pass. Let me try using `Box::leak(Box::new(...))` instead of raw alloc:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":